i've reset master to 171fdaa

from now on work in master (Projects/quicksilver) directly 

Two follow-ups, one of which blocks bifrost

1. The null gate applies a flat 5 s timeout to every test — this will fail on bifrost.

null_schema_gate builds its dummy with tests: Vec::new() (exec/schema.rs:628). In run_mutant_tests_with_env, that makes selected_sum = 0, so timeout = max(timeout_floor, 3 × 0) = 5 s flat, applied per test — while the empty list simultaneously means "run all staged tests."

bifrost's own .config/nextest.toml budgets 60s × terminate-after 10 and its comment treats tests running "over 60 seconds" as normal. So the first slow test trips the 5 s cap → Timeout → bail!("null-schema gate failed") → every mutant in that crate marked Unviable. The gate would be reporting a transform bug that doesn't exist.

identity_gate gets this right — it builds a real tests list, so its timeout scales. Apply the same shape: give the null gate the union of the crate's selected tests instead of an empty vec. Same test selection (that union is what's staged), correct timeout, and any entry that wasn't staged is dropped by the existing filter_map.

2. Worker cloning is now eager per slot, not lazy per job. ensure_worker runs for all build_jobs slots before the receive loop, so the fixture went 3 → 15 clones for 3 crates. On bifrost with 20 crates it's a win (20 → 15), but the first thing you'll actually run is run --schema -p brokk-bifrost-core — one crate, and it would clone a warm multi-GB target 15 times over NFS.

Clone lazily on first job instead:

let mut worker: Option<PathBuf> = None;
while let Ok((package, planned)) = job_rx.recv() {
    let w = match &worker {
        Some(w) => w.clone(),
        None => { let w = ensure_worker(...)?; worker = Some(w.clone()); w }
    };

That gives min(build_jobs, n_crates) clones. v1's exec/mod.rs has the identical pattern, so it's worth fixing in both — it's pre-existing there, not something A–D introduced.

then commit again
